## Why This Is Asked

This question tests expertise with closures, per-id memoization, and real-time rendering patterns in frontend systems. It specifically targets race-condition handling and efficient UI updates under high-frequency data scenarios.

## Key Concepts

- Closure-scoped per-id caches for memory efficiency
- Per-id memoization with bounded history management
- Promise chaining to serialize renders and handle race conditions
- Debounce using requestAnimationFrame for smooth UI updates

## Code Example

```javascript
function createDataUpdater() {
  const cache = new Map(); // id -> [last 5 results]
  let renderPromise = Promise.resolve();
  let renderScheduled = false;

  const updateCache = (id, data) => {
    const history = cache.get(id) || [];
    history.push(data);
    if (history.length > 5) history.shift();
    cache.set(id, history);
  };

  const render = () => {
    renderScheduled = false;
    // Apply cache data to UI
  };

  const scheduleRender = () => {
    if (!renderScheduled) {
      renderScheduled = true;
      requestAnimationFrame(render);
    }
  };

  return {
    processBatch: (batch) => {
      renderPromise = renderPromise.then(() => {
        batch.forEach(item => updateCache(item.id, item.data));
        scheduleRender();
      });
    }
  };
}
```

## Trade-offs

**Pros:**
- Closure ensures encapsulation and prevents external cache mutation
- Bounded cache prevents memory leaks from unbounded growth
- Promise chain guarantees render ordering consistency
- requestAnimationFrame provides optimal browser rendering timing

**Cons:**
- Added complexity compared to simple direct updates
- Memory overhead from maintaining per-id histories
- Potential render lag if batches arrive faster than processing capacity
- Cache management adds computational overhead per item

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests understanding of dynamic imports, caching strategies, concurrency handling, and import maps in the browser. It evaluates the ability to handle circular dependencies and cross-origin concerns while maintaining strict singleton semantics.

## Key Concepts

- Dynamic import() and module namespace objects
- Per-origin caching with inFlight deduplication
- Circular dependency handling without deadlocks
- Import maps specifier remapping
- Preload strategy and race condition management

## Code Example

```javascript
// Per-origin cache keyed by origin + URL
const moduleCache = new Map();
const inFlight = new Map();

async function loadModule(url, importMap) {
  const cacheKey = new URL(url).origin + new URL(url).pathname;

  if (moduleCache.has(cacheKey)) {
    return moduleCache.get(cacheKey);
  }

  if (inFlight.has(cacheKey)) {
    return inFlight.get(cacheKey);
  }

  const loadPromise = performLoad(url, importMap, cacheKey);
  inFlight.set(cacheKey, loadPromise);

  const module = await loadPromise;
  moduleCache.set(cacheKey, module);
  inFlight.delete(cacheKey);

  return module;
}

async function performLoad(url, importMap, cacheKey) {
  if (importMap) {
    const script = document.createElement('script');
    script.type = 'importmap';
    script.textContent = JSON.stringify(importMap);
    document.head.appendChild(script);
  }

  return import(url);
}

function preload(url, importMap) {
  return loadModule(url, importMap);
}
```

## Minimal A/B Circular Import Example

```javascript
// a.js
export const a = 'module A';
import('./b.js').then(b => console.log(b.b));

export const a2 = 'also A';

// b.js
export const b = 'module B';
import('./a.js').then(a => console.log(a.a));

export const b2 = 'also B';
```

The circular dependency resolves because JavaScript exports are hoisted—both modules complete loading, and the exported values become available once both module records are initialized.

